WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.313
Welcome to Building Azure
Security, Chapter 5:

2
00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:06.662
Front Door, Application
Gateway and the origin.

3
00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:10.587
The service tag tells an origin
a request came from a Front Door.

4
00:00:10.587 --> 00:00:13.438
Only the header tells it
the request came from yours.

5
00:00:13.438 --> 00:00:17.991
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's two
ways to lock an origin to the edge,

6
00:00:17.991 --> 00:00:19.639
see what they leave to you,

7
00:00:19.639 --> 00:00:24.427
read how the kit checks each origin's
configuration and where it stops and

8
00:00:24.427 --> 00:00:28.901
says "test this by hand", then walk
two labs, the evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:31.585
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

10
00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:35.890
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

11
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.790
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.352
A web application
firewall at the edge only

13
00:00:43.352 --> 00:00:46.128
helps if nothing can reach
the origin around it.

14
00:00:46.128 --> 00:00:50.252
By the end of this chapter you can
say, for every Front Door origin,

15
00:00:50.252 --> 00:00:54.222
whether its configuration lets
only your Front Door reach it;

16
00:00:54.222 --> 00:00:58.658
which origin groups can't fail over
or mix private and public origins;

17
00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:02.861
which custom domains or application
gateways allow TLS below 1.2;

18
00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:06.052
and which classic profiles
still need migrating.

19
00:01:06.052 --> 00:01:09.915
It's for application owners, who
own origins and certificates,

20
00:01:09.915 --> 00:01:12.490
and platform owners, who own the profiles.

21
00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.034
One limit to keep in mind:
the kit reads configuration,

22
00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:18.128
and the guide's test is a request.

23
00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:20.980
You'll know which answers
still need that test.

24
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.755
Here's what Microsoft Learn gives you.

25
00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:31.730
The origin security page says to use the
Azure Front Door Backend service tag and

26
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.734
the X-Azure-FDID header together,

27
00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:37.892
because the tag's addresses are
shared by every Front Door customer.

28
00:01:37.892 --> 00:01:42.118
Learn's App Service tutorial does
both in one access restriction.

29
00:01:42.118 --> 00:01:45.720
Where the origin supports it,
Private Link is the alternative:

30
00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.169
Front Door Premium requests a private
endpoint connection that the origin

31
00:01:50.169 --> 00:01:54.618
owner approves, and an origin group
shouldn't mix public and private origins.

32
00:01:54.618 --> 00:01:58.401
The App Service configuration
and Front Door origins APIs

33
00:01:58.401 --> 00:02:02.030
return what the kit reads, and
the header matches exactly.

34
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.680
On TLS, 1.2 or 1.3 only.

35
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.630
And classic Front Door
retires on March 31, 2027.

36
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.743
What does Learn leave to you?

37
00:02:15.743 --> 00:02:20.980
The command adds a restriction, but nothing
reads back what every origin has today.

38
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.692
A rule with the tag alone admits
any customer's Front Door.

39
00:02:24.692 --> 00:02:29.131
A header copied from another profile
admits that profile, not this one.

40
00:02:29.131 --> 00:02:33.044
An allow rule for any address,
or a default action of allow,

41
00:02:33.044 --> 00:02:36.042
opens the origin whatever
the tag rule says.

42
00:02:36.042 --> 00:02:41.442
"Don't mix origins" is a sentence, not
a check, and one origin can't fail over.

43
00:02:41.442 --> 00:02:45.131
And origins of other types
restrict access their own ways.

44
00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.848
The kit answers each,
and says plainly which

45
00:02:47.848 --> 00:02:51.160
origins it couldn't check,
so a person tests them.

46
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.485
Here's how the kit decides.

47
00:02:56.485 --> 00:02:59.010
Every origin gets one lock-down state.

48
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.810
Private Link, when the shared
private link connection is approved.

49
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.829
Service tag plus Front Door ID,

50
00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:10.238
when an App Service origin has an allow rule
for the tag with this profile's header,

51
00:03:10.238 --> 00:03:13.710
no allow rule for any address,
and no default of allow.

52
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.410
Those two count as locked.

53
00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.598
Tag only, when the header's missing.

54
00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:20.835
Other Front Door, when the
header is some other profile's.

55
00:03:20.835 --> 00:03:25.885
Open, when there's no tag rule, an
allow-any rule, or a default of allow.

56
00:03:25.885 --> 00:03:29.320
And unchecked, for any origin
type the kit can't read:

57
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.140
it's not counted as locked by anybody.

58
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.765
Beyond each origin, five more rules.

59
00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:42.815
A classic Front Door profile is
a finding, because it retires.

60
00:03:42.815 --> 00:03:45.848
An origin group that
mixes Private Link and

61
00:03:45.848 --> 00:03:48.965
public origins is a
finding, as Learn advises.

62
00:03:48.965 --> 00:03:53.934
A custom domain whose minimum TLS is
below 1.2 is a finding, and so is an

63
00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:57.129
application gateway whose TLS policy isn't

64
00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:00.590
one of the two predefined
policies Learn names.

65
00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:03.572
An origin group with
fewer than two enabled

66
00:04:03.572 --> 00:04:06.140
origins is a warning: it can't fail over.

67
00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.389
The origin groups' and custom
domains' calls are still unverified,

68
00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:14.520
read by their Resource Manager
names, which Lab 2 confirms.

69
00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:19.320
Who's involved?

70
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.379
The application owner owns the
origins and their certificates,

71
00:04:23.379 --> 00:04:26.595
and runs the direct-request
test the guide asks for.

72
00:04:26.595 --> 00:04:31.757
The platform owner owns the Front Door
profiles and the gateways' TLS policy.

73
00:04:31.757 --> 00:04:34.433
Developers keep the
lock-down states honest,

74
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:38.845
and make sure the unchecked list stays
visible, so nobody counts it as done.

75
00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:42.520
Every read the check makes is
Reader, and it sends nothing.

76
00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:51.570
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with Pester, in
the kit's folder, and no subscription.

77
00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.913
Contoso's Premium profile
has two origin groups:

78
00:04:54.913 --> 00:05:00.345
one with two App Service origins, one with
a Private Link origin and a public one.

79
00:05:00.345 --> 00:05:03.832
The Standard careers profile
has a single storage origin.

80
00:05:03.832 --> 00:05:09.258
Step 1 runs the edge check: two of
five origins locked by configuration.

81
00:05:09.258 --> 00:05:11.633
Step 2 reads each origin.

82
00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:16.408
The east origin has the tag and the
header; the west origin has the tag only.

83
00:05:16.408 --> 00:05:20.908
The private API origin reaches
its origin over Private Link.

84
00:05:20.908 --> 00:05:22.740
And two are unchecked.

85
00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.740
Step 3 reads the findings.

86
00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.977
The API origin group mixes a
private and a public origin.

87
00:05:32.977 --> 00:05:35.564
The west origin's tag rule has no header.

88
00:05:35.564 --> 00:05:39.565
The careers custom domain allows TLS 1.0.

89
00:05:39.565 --> 00:05:44.465
The intranet gateway uses
an old 2015 TLS policy.

90
00:05:44.465 --> 00:05:48.502
The careers origin group has one
origin, so it can't fail over.

91
00:05:48.502 --> 00:05:50.602
The classic profile is still there.

92
00:05:50.602 --> 00:05:54.226
And the two unchecked origins
get a warning that says,

93
00:05:54.226 --> 00:05:57.191
in plain words, test a
direct request by hand.

94
00:05:57.191 --> 00:06:00.220
Step 4 runs this chapter's two tests.

95
00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:07.183
Lab 2 moves to a test
subscription with a Front Door

96
00:06:07.183 --> 00:06:11.007
Premium profile and an App
Service origin, never production.

97
00:06:11.007 --> 00:06:14.052
Sign in to commercial
Azure, write the settings,

98
00:06:14.052 --> 00:06:17.721
and run the edge check live,
allowing the unverified calls.

99
00:06:17.721 --> 00:06:21.601
Compare the origin's access
restrictions with what the kit read,

100
00:06:21.601 --> 00:06:24.970
and check each custom domain's
minimum TLS in the portal.

101
00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:30.182
Then run the guide's own test: a direct
request to the origin should come back 403,

102
00:06:30.182 --> 00:06:33.870
and the same request through
Front Door should succeed.

103
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:38.320
If the kit said locked and the direct
request isn't refused, record it.

104
00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.733
Back out by deleting what the
lab wrote, and signing out.

105
00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:44.080
The authors haven't run this lab.

106
00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.495
Each output is evidence
for something specific,

107
00:06:50.495 --> 00:06:55.892
from the guide's requirements
table against NIST SP 800-53.

108
00:06:55.892 --> 00:06:59.786
Each origin's lock-down, read
from configuration and backed

109
00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:03.281
by Lab 2's request test,
supports boundary protection.

110
00:07:03.281 --> 00:07:07.497
TLS 1.2 as the minimum on
every custom domain and gateway

111
00:07:07.497 --> 00:07:11.805
supports transmission
confidentiality and integrity.

112
00:07:11.805 --> 00:07:13.885
A second origin in every group,

113
00:07:13.885 --> 00:07:17.630
with the failover test,
supports the contingency plan.

114
00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.367
And classic profiles named before their
retirement support boundary protection too.

115
00:07:22.367 --> 00:07:26.417
The kit's output supports these
controls; the assessor decides.

116
00:07:26.417 --> 00:07:31.168
And the key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers,

117
00:07:31.168 --> 00:07:34.380
which the agency lines
its own evidence up with.

118
00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.530
What breaks in production?

119
00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.226
The service tag alone lets any customer's

120
00:07:42.226 --> 00:07:45.080
Front Door reach the
origin: the kit flags it.

121
00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.535
A header copied from another
profile lets that profile in,

122
00:07:48.535 --> 00:07:51.845
so the kit compares the header
with this profile's own ID.

123
00:07:51.845 --> 00:07:56.523
Configuration taken as proof is a danger
of its own: a rule can read right while a

124
00:07:56.523 --> 00:08:01.417
request still gets through another path,
like another slot or the deployment site.

125
00:08:01.417 --> 00:08:06.829
So the metric says "by configuration",
and Lab 2's direct request is the test.

126
00:08:06.829 --> 00:08:11.291
And an origin the kit can't read would
otherwise be counted locked by nobody

127
00:08:11.291 --> 00:08:15.750
and tested by nobody, so it's flagged
every week until someone tests it.

128
00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:20.850
Here's what done looks like.

129
00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:25.649
Every origin is locked by Private Link
or by the tag and this profile's header,

130
00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:29.000
or has a decision record
with a compensating control.

131
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.700
Every unchecked origin has
a dated direct-request test.

132
00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.762
Every origin group has a second origin,
and failover was tested this half year.

133
00:08:37.762 --> 00:08:42.237
No custom domain or gateway
allows TLS below 1.2.

134
00:08:42.237 --> 00:08:49.376
And no classic profile remains, or its
migration is scheduled before March 31, 2027.

135
00:08:49.376 --> 00:08:55.520
Next, Chapter 6 turns from what comes in
to what leaves: Azure Firewall and egress.
